{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And if a man strikes his servant or his maidservant with a rod.</b> The servant and maidservahnt mentioned in the verse without qualification — the verse speaks of Canaanite slaves, and furthermore it is explicit: 'for he is his money.'" "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, explained that the word בשבט is mentioned with the definite article (בלשון ידיעה) because it is the way of a ruler and master to have a rod in his hand. And Scripture warns that even though it is a rod of discipline, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 22:15): ״שבט מוסר ירחיקנה ממנו״ — still he must not be quick to strike; he must guard against it and not strike with it even the Canaanite slave without cause. And this is the meaning of ״under his hand״ — that the blow was severe to the point of death. And it says ״נקום ינקם״ and did not need to specify the punishment explicitly, but says that he shall not be exempt because he is his money; rather, ״נקום ינקם״ — vengeance shall be taken — as the vengeance for any blow upon a man who dies, concerning whom it says ״מות יומת״. And the meaning of ״נקם ינקם״ is an obligation of death by the sword, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 26): ״חרב נקמת וגו׳״." "<b>And Rabbeinu Chananel,</b> of blessed memory, explained that the reason Scripture expressed it in the language of vengeance is that the master intended to kill him — when he dies under his hand, it comes about by way of cunning, like a person who seeks to take vengeance against his enemy, not intending to kill him outright. But since his intention was to kill him, therefore Scripture expressed the master's death in the language of vengeance, meaning: even the master — ״נקם ינקם״, vengeance shall be taken upon him."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:41.629535274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}